Content:
We would like the prove the following result:

Content:
[MATH: \dfrac{\textrm{d}}{\textrm{d}x}(\sinh{x}) = \cosh x]

Content:
To do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:

Content:
[MATH: \sinh x = \dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).]

Content:
Differentiating the above equation with respect to [MATH: x,] we get

Content:
[MATH: (d)/(dx)(\sinh x)|= (d)/(dx)((1)/(2)(e^{x} − e^{− x})); = (1)/(2) ⋅ (d)/(dx)(e^{x} − e^{− x}); = (1)/(2)(e^{x} + e^{− x}); = \cosh x.]

Content:
The proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself.

