Content:
We would like the prove the following result:

Content:
[MATH: \dfrac{\textrm d}{\textrm d x}(\tanh{x}) = \sech^2{x}]

Content:
We do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is

Content:
[MATH: \tanh{x} = \dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}.]

Content:
Differentiating the above equation with respect to [MATH: x] using the quotient rule, we arrive at

Content:
[MATH: (d)/(dx)(\tanh x)|= (d)/(dx)((e^{2x} − 1)/(e^{2x} + 1)); = ((e^{2x} − 1)^{′} ⋅ (e^{2x} + 1) − (e^{2x} − 1) ⋅ (e^{2x} + 1)^{′})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}); = ((2e^{2x})(e^{2x} + 1) − (e^{2x} − 1)(2e^{2x}))/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}); = (2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x} − 2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}); = (4e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}); = ((2e^{x})/(e^{2x} + 1))^{2}; = ((2)/(e^{x} + e^{− x}))^{2}; = sech^{2} x.]

